The yield parameters of an iterator are now checked for auto-initialization after the resolver has found how each datatype is constructed. The check ran while the resolver was still resolving the signatures of the module's declarations, before it had chosen any datatype's grounding constructor, the constructor the compiler uses for the default value, so it followed another constructor. A yield parameter of a datatype whose first constructor is recursive, as in `datatype L = Cons(int, L) | Nil`, was rejected or overflowed the stack; it is now accepted. A yield parameter of a datatype declared after the iterator, or inherited by a refining module, crashed the resolver; it is now checked like any other. A yield parameter of a datatype whose first constructor has a compilable value but whose grounding constructor has none, as in `datatype D<T> = C1(t: T) | C2(s: S)` with `type S = x: int | x > 0 witness *`, was accepted, though the compiled iterator left it null, so a verified program could print `null` or fail with a null reference; it is now rejected, as a class field of that type is, and so is a ghost yield parameter of such a type, as a ghost field is. The hidden option `--legacy-early-yield-parameter-check` restores the old, unsound check, for comparison and migration only (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/445).
